     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Two things stopped me.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. That is double the maximum labeled dose, which is 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release and 2,000 mg/day for extended-release. A single 1700 mg dose is also well above the usual maximum of 850\u20131000 mg per administration. A dose this high greatly raises the risk of lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **The order needs a prescriber.** You've said you aren't a clinician. Medication orders should come from a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient, and they can confirm the intended dose.\n\nThe prescriber may have meant a different dose. For example, 850 mg three times daily is 2,550 mg/day, and 1700 mg/day is a common total daily dose. If they confirm the intended dose and frequency, I can enter it right away. Or they can enter it themselves."
      }
     ],
